{
  "id": "make-the-string-great",
  "questionId": "make-the-string-great",
  "slug": "make-the-string-great",
  "title": "Make the String Great",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Monotonic stack",
  "patterns": [
    "stack",
    "string"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "a bad pair can only ever involve two characters that are directly adjacent in the current string - and after a removal, the two characters that become newly adjacent are the ones right before and after the removed pair",
    "what data structure naturally gives you 'the character that would become adjacent to the next one' after removals, without rebuilding the string each time?",
    "walk through s once, pushing each character onto a stack; before pushing, check if it forms a bad pair with the character currently on top of the stack",
    "if it's a bad pair, pop the stack instead of pushing (this represents both characters cancelling out); otherwise push normally. the final stack contents, in order, are the answer"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "checking character equality only, without checking that the cases are opposite (so 'aa' would incorrectly be treated as a bad pair)",
    "rebuilding the string with slicing on every removal instead of using a stack, causing quadratic behavior",
    "comparing ASCII values incorrectly when checking for 'same letter, opposite case'"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a string `s` made up of upper and lower case English letters.\nA pair of adjacent characters is \"bad\" if they're the same letter but\nopposite case (like 'a' next to 'A').\n\n**Repeatedly remove any adjacent bad pair until none remain, then return the resulting string.**\n\nRemoving a pair can create a new adjacent bad pair, which should also be\nremoved.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= s.length <= 100",
      "s consists of upper and lower case English letters"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "removing one pair exposes a new adjacent pair that also needs removing",
      "the whole string cancels out to empty",
      "no bad pairs exist anywhere in the string"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"leEeetcode\"",
        "output": "\"leetcode\"",
        "expectedOutput": "\"leetcode\"",
        "explanation": "the adjacent 'E' and 'e' cancel out, leaving leetcode, which has no more bad pairs"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"abBAcC\"",
        "output": "\"\"",
        "expectedOutput": "\"\"",
        "explanation": "abBAcC -> aAcC (removing bB) -> cC (removing aA) -> \"\" (removing cC)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"s\"",
        "output": "\"s\"",
        "expectedOutput": "\"s\"",
        "explanation": "a single character has no adjacent pair to remove"
      }
    ],
